window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-24","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-24-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-01/","title_he":"א – קֹסֵם קְסָמִים","title_en":"Kosem Kesamim (Practising Divination)","body_en":"<p>Kosem kesamim (one who practises divination) is one of the prohibitions of sorcery, as it is stated (Devarim 18:9-13): \"When you come into the land that the Lord your God is giving you, you shall not learn to do according to the abominations of those nations. There shall not be found among you anyone who passes his son or his daughter through the fire, one who practises divination, a soothsayer, a diviner of omens, or a sorcerer… For whoever does these things is an abomination to the Lord, and because of these abominations the Lord your God is driving them out from before you. You shall be wholehearted with the Lord your God.\" One who transgresses the prohibition and practises divination is liable to lashes.</p><p>The aim of the diviner (kosem) is to know the future. To this end he would perform various acts by means of which he disconnected himself from his surroundings and from his bodily sensations and sank into a meditative-hypnotic state of consciousness, while trying to connect with higher powers. From within this state he would guess the future, or decide whether or not it was worthwhile to do a certain thing. Some did this by repeatedly striking with a stick while making strange sounds; some did it by stirring sand while forming various shapes; some made use of stones or sand; some gazed at length into a mirror or into the flame of a lamp; and some bowed down to the ground while making strange movements (see Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:6; Sefer Ha-ḥinukh 510).</p><p>There were people who made use of divination without going to an expert diviner. In one widespread form of divination, they would take a branch and peel one side of it; the side with the bark they called \"man,\" and the peeled side \"woman.\" If they wished to set out on a journey, they would throw the stick twice: if \"man\" came up first and then \"woman,\" they decided that the journey was good and set out. If \"woman\" came up and then \"man,\" they decided that the journey was not good and did not set out. And if \"man\" came up twice or \"woman\" twice, they said it was an \"average journey.\" Concerning this the prophet rebuked them (Hoshe'a 4:12): \"My people consults its piece of wood, and its staff declares to it; for a spirit of harlotry has led them astray, and they have strayed from under their God\" (SMaG, negative commandment 52; Bekhor Shor and Hadar Zekenim on Devarim 18:10).</p><p>One of the famous diviners was Bil'am, who, in addition to his ability to curse by his sorcery, would foretell the future through his divinations. Therefore, when Balak wished to harm Israel, he sent messengers to Bil'am with instruments of divination, as it is stated: \"And the elders of Moav and the elders of Midyan went with divinations in their hand\" (Bamidbar 22:7; Rashi). This was because he feared that Bil'am might be afraid to curse Israel, and so that Bil'am could not evade his messengers on the pretext that he had no instruments of divination, he placed instruments of divination in their hands, by means of which Bil'am could determine the right time to curse them (Ibn Ezra). In the end, however, the Lord turned his curse into a blessing, and the Children of Israel took their vengeance on him, as it is stated (Yehoshua 13:22): \"And Bil'am the son of Be'or, the diviner, the Children of Israel slew with the sword among the rest of their slain.\"</p><p>One who seeks the counsel of a diviner also transgresses a prohibition, for he neglects the positive commandment by which the Torah commanded Israel to conduct themselves with wholeheartedness, as it is stated (Devarim 18:13): \"You shall be wholehearted with the Lord your God.\" That is, to believe that the Lord watches over man and that all His governance is for man's good; therefore one must not try to know the future by the crooked ways of divination and tricks (above 19:12-13). However, the diviner, who performs the divination himself, also transgresses a negative prohibition (lav). And some say that one who consults a diviner also transgresses a negative prohibition.<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"There are two main opinions as to the definition of the prohibition of turning to sorcerers, diviners, soothsayers (me'onenim) and diviners of omens (menaḥashim):<br><b>a)</b> Some say that one who consults a diviner also transgresses a negative prohibition (lav), but he does not receive lashes for it by Torah law, because it is a lav that involves no action. This emerges from Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:7): \"It is forbidden to practise divination or to consult a diviner; but one who consults a diviner is given lashes of rebellion (makkat mardut), whereas the diviner himself, if he performed any one of these acts or the like, receives lashes.\" And if he timed his work according to the diviner, since he performed an action, he receives lashes. As he wrote (ibid. 11:9): \"Whoever does something on account of astrology, timing his work or his journey to the time set by the stargazers, receives lashes, as it is stated: 'and you shall not practise soothsaying' (lo te'onenu).\" So wrote Migdal Oz, Leḥem Mishneh, Beit Ya'akov of Mahari Bei Rav, and Be'erot Ha-mayim (on Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:7). So too wrote Responsa Rashbash 610. <b>b)</b> Some say that one who consults a diviner, soothsayer or diviner of omens does not transgress a negative prohibition, because the lav applies only to the diviner, soothsayer or diviner of omens himself; but one who consults them neglects the positive commandment of \"You shall be wholehearted.\" So wrote Terumat Ha-deshen (Pesakim 96) and Maharshal (Responsa 3). This is also the opinion of Ramban (Responsa attributed to him, 283), Rivash (92) and the Ḥinukh (510). Many also explained Rambam this way (Mirkevet Ha-mishneh of Ḥelma, Seder Mishneh, Avodat Ha-melekh), and they strengthened their view from the fact that Rambam wrote in Sefer Ha-mitzvot (negative commandment 31) that one who consults a diviner does not receive lashes, \"but consulting a diviner is very disgraceful,\" and he did not say that it involves a lav. This implies that he transgresses the Torah's instruction \"You shall be wholehearted\"; and although Rambam did not count it among the 248 positive commandments, many hold that he agrees that it is a Torah commandment (see above 19, note 11). (Mishneh Kesef explained that according to Rambam only one who consults a soothsayer or a diviner of omens transgresses a lav and is punished with lashes, because their prohibition is repeated in the book of Vayikra as well (19:26): \"You shall not practise divination by omens, nor practise soothsaying\"; but one who consults a diviner merely neglects a positive commandment and does not receive lashes.)"}]},{"slug":"20-24-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-02/","title_he":"ב – מְעוֹנֵן","title_en":"Me'onen (Soothsayer)","body_en":"<p>Included in the prohibitions of sorcery are the me'onen (soothsayer) and the menaḥesh (diviner of omens), as it is stated (Devarim 18:10): \"There shall not be found among you anyone who passes his son or his daughter through the fire, one who practises divination, a soothsayer, or a diviner of omens.\" It is likewise stated (Vayikra 19:26): \"You shall not eat over the blood; you shall not practise divination by omens, nor practise soothsaying.\" A me'onen is one who believes the sorcerers that there are good times and bad times, as will be explained further on in this halakha. A menaḥesh is one who believes that he can know, on the basis of events that happened to him by chance, what it is worthwhile for him to do, as will be explained in the next halakha.</p><p>In general, the superstitions of the me'onen and the menaḥesh were created by people with mystical intuition who engaged in sorcery and divination and sensed that certain things hint at danger or at success. Sometimes the divination was based on a natural feeling. For example, a person who began his business day by paying a high tax might become depressed and as a result fail in his business that day; the diviners concluded that paying a tax at the beginning of the day causes mystical forces to plot against him, and they therefore instructed people to avoid paying tax at the beginning of the day, the week or the month. Israel, however, are commanded to direct their faith to the Torah and the mitzvot, which elevate a person to do good deeds and to add blessing to the world, and not to turn to superstitions that have no rational basis.</p><p>Even when it appears that sometimes the sorcerers who engaged in divination succeeded in guessing the future, in truth they did not see the whole picture. Consequently, even if one who followed them gained in the short term, in the long term he lost twice over. First, relying on the instructions of sorcerers prevents a person from weighing rational considerations, from which more blessing grows for him. Second, one who follows them focuses all his attention, including his spiritual attention, on external success alone, and removes himself from the Torah, whose guidance is intended to elevate a person morally and to add blessing to his life in this world and in the World to Come.</p><p>Who is a me'onen? Rabbi Akiva explained: \"This is one who calculates times and hours, and says: Today is a good day to set out; tomorrow is a good day to buy (merchandise); the wheat of the eve of the Sabbatical years is fine (wheat that grows on the eve of the Shemita year tends to be fine).\" Me'onen derives from the word ona (a time or season), and one who sins in this way acts according to a superstition that certain times are good and certain times are bad (Sanhedrin 65b). Whoever performs an action on this basis is liable to lashes (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:9).</p><p>However, there is no prohibition in guidance of the Sages concerning times that is based on Torah values. Thus the Sages advised (Ta'anit 29b) a Jew who has a lawsuit with a gentile to avoid it during the month of Av, when his fortune (mazal) is bad, and to try to make himself available for the lawsuit or for business in the month of Adar, when the fortune of Israel is successful.<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"Similarly, we have learned: a widow marries on Thursday, since a blessing was said on it to the fish (Ketubot 5a); Shmuel advised letting blood only on Sunday, Wednesday and Friday (Shabbat 129b). Some hold that according to Rambam one should not follow these instructions and others like them (Maharsham 9:34; see Sho'el U-nish'al part 1, YD 51), but most poskim agreed to uphold them. Ritva (on Ta'anit ibid.) explained that in the months of Adar and Av Israel has a fortune, \"for so it was decreed upon us from Heaven,\" meaning that this is not a matter of the words of diviners and soothsayers but of Torah guidance. Along similar lines Tosafot and Maharsha wrote (on Ta'anit ibid.) that although the Sages said that Israel is not subject to mazal, merit is brought about through one who is meritorious and guilt through one who is guilty. Ranaḥ explained (Ha-noten Imrei Shefer, p. 613) that the me'onen attributes his success to mazal, whereas the Sages who said that merit is brought about through one who is meritorious attribute success to the Lord, \"for on a day of good the Lord too will give, and the attribute of mercy prevails then, and on a day of evil the opposite.\" So too wrote Rav Yitzḥak Maio (Sharshei Ha-yam, Avoda Zara, root 20; died 5570). So too wrote Rav Shlomo Eliezer Alfandari (Moriah 83-84; died 5690): \"As for the Torah's prohibition of sorcery and soothsaying, the reason is as the Ḥinukh wrote (in parashat Kedoshim and Shoftim), that it would lead them to deny the fundamental principle of faith; which is not the case with one who refrains in the month of Av and acts in the month of Adar – on the contrary, it is a cause for him to believe in the Lord.\" (See further below 25:5, note 3, that some hold that calculations based on the wisdom of astrology are not included in the prohibition of me'onen.)"}]},{"slug":"20-24-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-03/","title_he":"ג – מְנַחֵשׁ","title_en":"Menaḥesh (Diviner of Omens)","body_en":"<p>Menaḥesh derives from the word \"to hasten\" (Ramban). Naturally, a person does not know what will happen in the future until it actually happens, and the menaḥesh wants to hasten and know the future on the basis of events that are happening to him now, without there being any rational causal connection between what happened to him and what he guesses will be. The Sages brought examples (Sanhedrin 65b) of things forbidden under the prohibition of menaḥesh: \"One who says: his bread fell from his mouth\" – and on this basis worries about harm all that day. Likewise if \"his staff fell from his hand, his son calls him from behind, a raven calls to him, a deer cut across his path, a snake was on his right and a fox on his left,\" and he saw in this a bad sign, and on that basis refrained from setting out on a journey or from doing business as he had planned. Likewise \"those who divine by means of a weasel, birds, fish and stars\" transgress the prohibition of divining omens (Sanhedrin 66a). That is, there was a superstition that if a person saw certain things in birds, fish or stars, it would be a sign for him to do the business or set out on the journey, or to refrain from doing so.</p><p>There was likewise a superstition that one who pays the customs duty at the beginning of the day, or at the beginning of the month or of the week, will lose in his business. Therefore, when the tax collector comes to collect taxes, one who says to him: \"Do not start with me, (for) it is morning, it is Rosh Ḥodesh, it is Motza'ei Shabbat,\" transgresses the prohibition of divining omens (Sanhedrin 66a).</p><p>Similarly, included in the prohibition of divining omens, it is forbidden to perform acts based on superstitions practised by the gentiles. For example, those who believe that if a hen crows with the voice of a rooster it is a bad sign, and to be saved from it one must slaughter the hen. Likewise, people who raised chicks used to dash eggs against a wall as a charm (segula) for the chicks' survival, and there were women who for this purpose danced before the chicks and counted seventy-one of them. There were also customs of women connected to cooking food: when they prepared a kutaḥ dish, they danced before the dish so that it would turn out well; when they cooked lentils they silenced people so that they would not spoil the cooking; and when they cooked groats they shrieked so that they would cook well (Shabbat 67b).</p><p>Similarly, some fear a black cat that has passed in front of them, regarding it as a bad sign, and change their plans as a result. Some are afraid to go back into a house they have just left, and if they forgot something, they walk around a little and only then return home. Some are afraid to whistle at home, out of concern that the whistling will cause there to be no money in the house. Some avoid sitting at the corner of the table, lest whoever sits there not marry for seven years. Some avoid stepping over a baby or a child out of concern that his development will be harmed, and some are very careful not to break a mirror. And some wear a red thread as a charm. Whoever fears things such as these transgresses the prohibition of divining omens.</p><p>Some hold, however, that any custom that God-fearing Jews also feared is permitted. For example, it is permitted for one who has left the house to avoid going back into it out of fear of danger, and it is permitted to avoid stepping over a baby. In practice, however, it seems that since these are customs learned from the gentiles, and they have no rational basis, they are forbidden. True, in the past there were those who feared customs of this sort that were learned from the gentiles, but that was because they thought that they truly involved an occult (segulit) danger; today, however, when many know that they involve no danger, the law has reverted to its place, and they are forbidden because of divining omens. This is the rule: whoever performs an action or refrains from performing an action on the basis of superstitions transgresses the prohibition of divining omens.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Simply understood, all the acts the Sages enumerated with regard to the prohibition of menaḥesh are only examples. Therefore, just as one who fears a fox passing on his left transgresses the prohibition of divining omens (Sanhedrin 65b), so one who fears a black cat passing in front of him transgresses the prohibition of divining omens. And just as one who refrains from paying tax at the beginning of the week transgresses the prohibition of divining omens, so one who refrains from sitting at the corner of the table or refrains from going back into the house he left transgresses the prohibition of divining omens. One could understand the prohibition of divining omens as including only things that a person refrains from doing after he saw a certain thing, or in order to prevent danger. But in my humble opinion it is more correct that it includes every performance of an action or refraining from an action on the basis of a superstition; and then all the things that are forbidden because of \"the ways of the Emori\" (darkhei ha-Emori), that is, things that are superstitions and were learned from the gentiles, as brought in tractate Shabbat 67b, are also forbidden because of divining omens, and from them too one can learn examples of the prohibition of divining omens.<br>In the past there were those who held that things of this sort are dangerous, as written in the Testament of Rabbi Yehuda He-ḥasid (43): one who leaves his house for a journey and forgot something should not go back into his house, but should stand outside and ask that it be brought out to him (cited in Kaf Ha-ḥayim 110:21). Likewise, some learned from Rashba that one should uphold the customs of the old men and women even if their reason is not known (Responsa 1:9). On this basis some wrote of further concerns: Minḥat Yitzḥak 9:8 was concerned about the custom not to eat from the ends of the bread. Responsa Be'er Moshe 8:36 was concerned about what people were accustomed to do: not to let a child whose teeth have not yet come in look in a mirror, not to step over a small child lest he not grow, and to tie a red thread on a baby's carriage. It seems that they based their words on the view of Ramban and those who agree with him, who held that although it is forbidden to consult astrologers, if it becomes known that in their opinion a certain thing is dangerous, it is permitted to be concerned about it, for sometimes there is truth in their words (below 25:5, note 3). And so people acted with regard to other customs learned from the gentiles: since they held that the matter involved a concern of danger, there was no prohibition of divining omens in it.<br>In practice, however, it seems that it is forbidden to be concerned about these things. First, according to Rambam and those who agree with him, the words of the astrologers too are nonsense, and it is forbidden to be concerned about their words because of \"you shall not practise soothsaying\" (ibid.). And it seems that according to them all the customs brought above and others like them are forbidden because of me'onen or menaḥesh. And even if we assume that Ramban and those who agree with him disagree with Rambam and those who agree with him, with a Torah prohibition it is right to be concerned for the stringent view. Second, it seems that today they are forbidden even according to Ramban and those who agree with him, for they permitted being concerned about them because they held that sometimes there is truth in the words of the astrologers and soothsayers; but today, when we know that customs such as those detailed above are superstitions, their law has reverted to that of all the instructions of soothsayers and diviners of omens, which are forbidden because of me'onen and menaḥesh, in accordance with the words of Rambam and those who agree with him (see below 26:11-12 and the notes there, that there are customs that people continue to observe out of respect for earlier generations). However, one should not rebuke someone who observes them, for he has something on which to rely. And the less he believes in these things, the more the concern of a prohibition diminishes. But it is not right to write in books that one should be careful about this, similar to what the poskim ruled, not to rely on the remedies in the Talmud (below 29:5).<br>In Tosefta Shabbat (6:1; 7:11) it is brought that it is forbidden to tie a red thread on the finger because of \"the ways of the Emori.\" Following from this, it was said in the name of Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach that one should not wear a red thread taken from Rachel's Tomb (Ḥikko Mamtakim 1, p. 281; there were, however, rabbis who permitted it). As for the \"ḥamsa\" (the shape of a palm with five fingers), some regarded it as a kind of amulet which, when the letter heh (standing for the Name of God) is written in it, can serve as a charm against the evil eye (Ben Ish Ḥai, second year, Pinḥas 13, in the name of the Ḥida); and then, according to all those who hold that one should not use an amulet, one should not use it (above 20:8)."}]},{"slug":"20-24-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-04/","title_he":"ד – ניחוש אסור סימן מותר","title_en":"Divining Omens Is Forbidden, a Sign Is Permitted","body_en":"<p>The Sages said: although divining omens (niḥush) is forbidden, a sign (siman) is permitted (Ḥullin 95b). For example, a person who bought a house, married a wife or had a child born to him, and since then succeeded in his business three times, may see this as a good sign that he has entered a good period of success, and be encouraged to increase his business dealings. Conversely, if since then he lost in his business three times, he may see this as a bad sign that this period is not successful for him, and that he would do better to reduce his business dealings.</p><p>But all this is on condition that he treats the sign as a possibility that may perhaps materialise, and not as a forecast that he believes will materialise, so that his main consideration will be according to reason and not according to the sign. Thus, if by his understanding the deal he is interested in making is good, he will not refrain from making it even if he had a bad sign, and likewise the reverse. For one may make use of a sign as an indication of direction that joins the main consideration; but if he treats the sign as a forecast that he believes will materialise, the sign will become the main reason for his doing business or refraining from it, and he will transgress the prohibition of menaḥesh.</p><p>That is to say, events that have happened to a person contain a sign and an expression of a certain atmosphere in which he finds himself, positive or negative, and one may pay attention to this. But the diviners of omens exaggerated the importance of signs and conducted their lives according to them, and thereby transgressed the prohibition of divining omens. All the more so, it is forbidden to act according to signs when taking them into account will harm the values of the Torah and its mitzvot.<sup>4</sup></p><p>Since signs have a certain value in creating a positive atmosphere that influences life for the good, the Sages recommended eating foods on Rosh Ha-shana that express a good sign for the whole year (Horayot 12a; SA OḤ 583:1-2; Peninei Halakha, Yamim Nora'im 3:12). Likewise, it is customary to say verses of blessing at Havdala as a good sign, and many are accustomed to fill the Havdala cup until it overflows, so that a little wine spills as a good sign of abundance (Rema OḤ 296:1). Similarly, it was the custom to anoint kings of the House of David beside a spring, as a sign of blessing that their kingship would continue (Horayot 12a). Similarly, those of Ashkenazi communities were accustomed to hold wedding canopies (ḥuppot) under the open sky, as a sign of blessing that their offspring would multiply like the stars of the heavens (Rema EH 61:1). Likewise, some were accustomed to begin studying a new tractate on Rosh Ḥodesh, as a good sign of renewal (Rema YD 179:2). But one must not neglect Torah study because of this custom (Sefer Ḥasidim 59).</p><p>Some were accustomed to schedule weddings in the first half of the month, on the days when the moon is waxing, and not in the second half of the month, when the moon is waning. Most of Israel, however, were not particular about this, for the rule is that one should not change plans or harm values because of signs, and avoiding scheduling weddings in the second half of the month could cause the mitzva of marriage to be postponed by several days and add to the costs.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Ḥullin 95b: \"It was taught: Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says: A house, a child and a wife – although there is no divination by omens (naḥash), there is a sign. Rabbi Elazar said: And this is when it has been established three times.\" Rashi explained: \"It is at least a mere sign: if he succeeds… after he built a house, or the child was born, or he married a wife – it is a sign that he is going on to succeed; and if not – he should not be accustomed to go out too much, for there is reason to fear that he will not succeed.\" This implies that he should not determine his actions according to the sign, for then he would be considered a menaḥesh; rather, he should only be encouraged to increase his business dealings according to the good sign, and to reduce them when the sign is bad. This is also the opinion of most of the Rishonim and poskim. According to Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:5), the entire permission is to note the good sign and rejoice in it, and nothing more. It seems that in practice the difference between them is small, for since according to Rambam it is permitted to note a good sign and rejoice in it, it stands to reason that this will influence him to strengthen himself a little in his business. Accordingly, even according to Rambam, if he emphasises that the sign is not the deciding consideration but only one of the considerations, or if he says that \"apparently\" there is a good sign, it will be permitted. And this is what I wrote above (and see Kesef Mishneh ibid.)."},{"n":5,"en":"In SA (YD) 179:2 the custom not to marry in the second half of the month is brought; its source is a responsum of Ramban (Responsa attributed to him, 283) and the New Responsa of Maharil 92. Ramban explained that it involves no prohibition of divining omens, since it is done as a \"good sign\" (simana tava). In practice, however, in most communities people were accustomed to marry in the second half of the month as well. Thus did Rav Ḥayim of Volozhin, who married off his son at the end of the month and said that one need not be particular about the times (Keter Rosh 118). So too wrote Arukh Ha-shulḥan (EH 64:13), that it is better not to be particular about it at all. Responsa Yehuda Ya'aleh (EH 2:24) wrote that even one who makes an effort to schedule a wedding in the first half of the month should be careful not to say so aloud, because according to Rambam it is forbidden. Some explained that the poskim merely permitted this custom and said that it does not involve divining omens, but did not recommend following it (Responsa Aryeh De-vei Ila'i, EH 18). In any case, when being particular about signs causes delay in a mitzva, one should refrain from taking them into account, as written in Sefer Ha-yir'a (§228): \"And if a person has a mitzva to do, such as reviewing a tractate or sending his son back to his studies, let him not say: I will wait until Rosh Ḥodesh or until Pesaḥ; for every day and every time is good to begin and to do mitzvot, and whoever is not particular – they will never be particular with him. But as for those who are particular, the Satan stands at their right hand to accuse them, to keep them in their error.\" It should be added that in the past weddings took place within the small community, and changing the date of the wedding did not require great effort; whereas today weddings are held with large crowds, in halls, and by means of several professionals such as musicians and photographers, and restricting the times of weddings to the first half of the month would make the cost of weddings very much more expensive. Regarding all the customs of signs practised in Israel, the Mordekhai (Yoma 723) brought the responsum of the Ge'onim, who explained that all of them express good values rooted in the Torah and in the words of Ḥazal. (See above, note 2.)"}]},{"slug":"20-24-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-05/","title_he":"ה – האם ניחוש לעתיד אסור (אליעזר ויהונתן)","title_en":"Is Divining by a Future Sign Forbidden? (Eliezer and Yehonatan)","body_en":"<p>Some say that there is no prohibition in setting a sign such that if it happens, one will carry out one's plan, and if not, one will abandon it, while believing that in this way one will succeed. For in their view the prohibition of divining omens is to act according to a sign that has already occurred, such as a deer that crossed one's path, so that one therefore decided not to set out on one's journey; but setting a sign for the future is permitted (Ra'avad). And although it is proper for a person to make his decisions according to reason and not according to random signs, this involves no prohibition of divining omens.</p><p>According to most of the Rishonim, however, just as it is forbidden to divine according to an incident that happened to a person, such as his bread falling or a fox passing on his left, while believing that if he acts according to these signs he will succeed in his path, so it is forbidden to divine according to a sign that he sets for himself in advance, while believing that in this way he will succeed in his business. For example, he sets for himself that if someone at a meeting wears a blue garment, he will agree to the deal, and if not, he will refuse it. That is, the rule in the prohibition of divining omens is that a person may not decide how to act according to random signs while believing that in this way he will succeed. Since this is the opinion of most of the Rishonim, and moreover this is a dispute concerning a Torah prohibition, this is how the halakha is ruled.</p><p>But if the sign one set for the future has a certain logic to it, even according to the stringent view there is no prohibition of divining omens. Thus did Eliezer, the servant of Avraham, who set for himself a sign that the young woman who would volunteer to take the trouble to give drink to him and his camels would be the woman whom \"the Lord has appointed\" as suitable for Yitzḥak, since this sign testifies to her goodness of heart. And although there is no prohibition in such a sign, it is not proper to act according to signs of this kind, as the Sages said (Ta'anit 4a) that Eliezer \"asked improperly,\" for had an unsuitable young woman volunteered to give him drink, according to the sign he would have been obligated to match her with Yitzḥak. Therefore, although one may set a sign that has logic to it, it is right to set it as a sign from which one draws inspiration, and not as a sign on which one relies.<sup>6</sup></p><p>According to all opinions there is no prohibition for one who finds it difficult to decide to set for himself a decision mechanism according to which he will act, such as deciding to act according to the toss of a coin. And although one who acts this way is not acting wisely, since he does not hold that the coin will predict his success, there is no prohibition of divining omens in this.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Ḥullin 95b: \"Rav said: Any divination (naḥash) that is not like that of Eliezer the servant of Avraham and Yonatan the son of Shaul is not divination,\" but if it is like that of Eliezer and Yehonatan, it is the forbidden divination. Eliezer the servant of Avraham went out on the mission of Avraham our father to find a wife for Yitzḥak, and set for himself a \"naḥash,\" that the young woman who would agree to give him drink and would also give drink to his camels would be the wife for Yitzḥak (Bereshit 24). Yehonatan the son of Shaul, in the days of the war against the Philistines, wished to go up with his attendant and attack the Philistine force, and set a \"naḥash\": if the Philistine guards would say, \"Come up to us,\" they would go up and fight them; but if they would say, \"Wait until we come to you,\" they would not go up (I Shmuel 14:9-10). That is, the forbidden \"naḥash\" is when one determines to perform an action according to a certain thing, as Eliezer and Yehonatan determined. But the Rishonim agreed that Eliezer and Yehonatan themselves did not transgress the prohibition. Many explained that the prohibition is to rely on a \"naḥash\" that has no logic to it, but their actions had logic: Eliezer tested the young woman's goodness of heart, and Yehonatan the courage of the Philistines, and therefore these are permitted signs (Ran, Ḥullin 95b; Abarbanel, Bereshit 24; and others). Some explain that Eliezer and Yehonatan did not transgress the prohibition of naḥash because they did not rely entirely on the sign they had set: for Eliezer gave Rivka the bracelets only after he learned that she was of Avraham's family, and Yehonatan would have gone up in any case and only wished to encourage his own spirit and that of his attendant by means of the sign (Tosafot, Ḥullin 95b, s.v. \"Ke-Eliezer\" and \"Ve-khi-Yonatan\"; Ritva ibid.; SMaG, negative commandment 51; and others). Some say that a naḥash like that of Eliezer and Yehonatan is forbidden, but they relied on the Lord, through prayer, that He would guide their way (Ḥovot Ha-levavot 5:5). According to the Riaz (Sanhedrin 7:13), the prohibition is only when one says aloud that he is refraining from something because of the sign (cited in Pitḥei Teshuva YD 179:3; so too wrote Torat Ḥayim, Bava Metzia 27b).<br>Some say that the prohibition of divining omens is to act according to a sign that has already occurred, whereas Eliezer and Yehonatan determined to act according to a sign that would occur in the future, and this is permitted. This is the opinion of Ra'avad (on Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:4-5), Radak (I Shmuel 14:9) and Meiri (Sanhedrin 68a). On the other hand, according to Rambam (ibid.), a sign concerning the future is also forbidden because of naḥash, and this is also the opinion of the Tur YD 179:5. This is also the opinion of all the Rishonim cited in the previous paragraph. The Rema (179:4) mentioned both opinions and concluded: \"And one who walks in wholeheartedness and trusts in the Lord – kindness will surround him,\" that is, it is right to act according to the stringent view. The Levush wrote to be stringent. On the other hand, the Gra (11) inclined to be lenient. Since the opinion of most of the Rishonim is to be stringent, and the doubt concerns a Torah prohibition, so is the halakha. And if he does not intend to rely entirely on the future sign, then even according to Rambam and those who agree with him there is no prohibition in it, as Kesef Mishneh wrote (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:4). And this is what I wrote above."},{"n":7,"en":"So wrote Yeri'ot Shlomo 1:4. This is also implied by Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:4) and Ran (Ḥullin 95b): the prohibition is when the diviner of omens holds that through it he will succeed even though there is no logic in it. This is also explained below at the end of note 11."}]},{"slug":"20-24-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-06/","title_he":"ו – שאלת פסוק מילד","title_en":"Asking a Child for a Verse","body_en":"<p>There were Sages who, when they were uncertain whether to set out on a journey or to do something, would ask a small child who was studying Scripture: \"Tell me your verse.\" That is, tell me the verse you learned today; and from the verse he said, they resolved their doubt. Likewise it is told of Rabbi Yoḥanan, who lived in the Land of Israel and was uncertain whether to go down to Babylonia to meet the Amora Shmuel, that in order to resolve his doubt he asked a child what verse he had learned. The child answered: \"And Shmuel died, and all Israel lamented him\" (I Shmuel 28:3). Rabbi Yoḥanan concluded from this that Shmuel had passed away, and he remained in the Land. The Gemara concludes, however, that in truth Shmuel was alive, but so that Rabbi Yoḥanan would not trouble himself to go down to Babylonia, this verse was arranged for him from Heaven (Ḥullin 95b).</p><p>The Rishonim explained that this incident involves no prohibition of divining omens, for the prohibition of divining omens is to rely on signs that have no logic to them, whereas asking a child for a verse involves a kind of \"minor prophecy.\" For the Sages said (Bava Batra 12b): \"From the day the Temple was destroyed, prophecy was taken from the prophets and given to fools and to children\" (small children). According to Rambam, however, it is forbidden to act according to what the child says; one may only rejoice if he says a good verse, and take encouragement from the fact that it is a good sign regarding something one has already done (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:5). But according to most poskim, it is permitted to act according to the verse the child says, on condition that the guidance emerging from the verse is not contrary to the guidance of the Torah or to reason. In practice, the vast majority of rabbis throughout all the generations did not customarily make use of \"asking for a verse,\" but rather acted according to their own judgement.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Ḥullin 95b: \"Rabbi Yoḥanan would test by means of a child,\" and the meaning is by \"asking for a verse\" (Rabbeinu Gershom and Rashi). We likewise find in Gittin 68a that Rav Sheshet feared the servants of the Exilarch, who wanted to kill him, and therefore asked a child for a verse; the child answered: \"Turn aside to your right or to your left\" (II Shmuel 2:21), and by virtue of this he turned aside and did not fall into the trap they had laid for him (for more on asking a child for a verse, see Ḥagiga 15a; Esther Rabba 7:17). So too wrote SMaG (negative commandment 51): \"We find several great men who said to a child: Tell me your verse… and they act according to the words of the verse and regard this as a kind of prophecy.\" So too wrote Ran, Ḥullin 95b; Meiri, Sanhedrin 68a; and others. According to Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:5), however, it is forbidden to rely on this in order to do something or refrain from it; one may only rejoice and say \"this is a good sign.\" Kesef Mishneh explained that Rav Sheshet, who asked a child for a verse, acted mainly according to his own understanding of the situation, and only took a little encouragement from the verse he heard. This also emerges from the Tur 179:4 and Sefer Ha-batim 33. According to the Baḥ 179:6, it is possible that even according to Rambam, Sages and righteous men such as Rabbi Yoḥanan and Rav Sheshet may ask a child, because \"all their actions and affairs are according to the holy spirit,\" and therefore their question is \"as if he had inquired of the word of the Lord.\" In practice, most Aḥaronim ruled that it is permitted to rely on \"asking for a verse,\" since it is considered a kind of prophecy (Levush 179:4; Perisha 11; Taz 3; Shakh 5; Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 166:2).<br>Regarding the prophecy in the words of children, it was said in Bava Batra 12b: \"From the day the Temple was destroyed, prophecy was taken from the prophets and given to fools and to children.\" Maharal explained (Netzaḥ Yisrael 26) that sometimes precisely one who lacks understanding is able to receive things from the higher realms in simplicity. Ḥatam Sofer likewise explained (Bava Batra 12b), \"for sometimes a person's soul sees things, and his heart tells him,\" but he refrains from saying them because in the eyes of his intellect it appears as foolishness; whereas the fool and the child say them without the barriers of the intellect."}]},{"slug":"20-24-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-07/","title_he":"ז – פתיחת ספר","title_en":"Opening a Book","body_en":"<p>Just as there were Sages who sometimes resolved a doubt by \"asking for a verse,\" so sometimes they resolved a doubt by opening a Torah scroll and looking at the verse that opened before them. Thus it is told in the Talmud (Ḥullin 95b) of the Amora Shmuel, that sometimes when he was uncertain, he would open a book to see what verse would come to him. As with asking for a verse, according to Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:5) it is forbidden to act according to this; one may only rejoice if he sees a good verse, and take encouragement from the fact that it is a good sign regarding something he has already done. According to most poskim, however, it is permitted to act according to the verse that came up before him, on condition that the guidance emerging from it is not contrary to the guidance of the Torah or to reason.<sup>9</sup></p><p>In recent generations, there were rabbis who used a sophisticated method of opening a book, called the \"Goral Ha-Gra\" (the lottery of the Vilna Gaon). According to it, one opens a Torah scroll or a Tanakh at random, counts seven pages from the place of opening, and looks at the eighth page, in the eighth line, at the eighth letter, and there one looks for an answer or a hint of an answer. It seems, however, that this method does not originate with the Gra, for none of his disciples mentioned it, and only several generations later was it mentioned in his name.</p><p>In practice, it is preferable not to use opening a book to make decisions, including the method called \"Goral Ha-Gra.\" First, the vast majority of rabbis of all generations did not make use of \"opening a book\" or \"asking for a verse,\" but weighed their actions in light of the guidance of the Torah as they understood it with their intellect; evidently they held that opening a book is a less good method of decision-making. Second, some say that it is forbidden to make decisions on the basis of opening a book (Rambam and Tur), and although most poskim are lenient, it is proper to be stringent from the outset (le-khatḥila). Only in special cases, when the matter does not concern decisions for the future but is meant to prevent anguish, is there perhaps room to use this method, as Rav Aryeh Levin of blessed memory did in order to set up gravestones for the holy martyrs of the Convoy of the Thirty-Five (Shayeret Ha-Lamed-Heh), who were killed in the war for the defence of the people and the Land.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Ḥullin 95b: \"Rav would test by means of a ferry (when he was uncertain whether to set out on a journey, if a ferry was available to him to cross the river, he would set out, and if not, not), Shmuel would test by means of a book (he would open a book), Rabbi Yoḥanan would test by means of a child.\" Rabbeinu Gershom explained: \"He would test by means of a book – whether it turned up a good verse or not.\" According to Rambam and Tur, it is forbidden to do something on the basis of such signs, but according to many it is permitted to resolve doubts and to act on this basis. As Meiri wrote (Sanhedrin 68a) on the basis of the practice of Shmuel, who \"tested by means of a book\": \"Certainly his test was not for nothing; rather, he would act or refrain\" according to that verse. According to Mahariks (Erekh Leḥem 179:4), even according to Rambam, who forbids making use of asking a child for a verse, it is permitted to make use of opening a Torah scroll, \"for it is our life.\" He brought proof from Yoshiyahu, in whose days the Torah scroll that was in the Temple was found, and according to tradition it was found open at a verse that hinted at calamity, and as a result he carried out a general reform in the kingdom. So too wrote the Ḥida in Shiyurei Berakha 179:6, in the name of Mahariks and Rav Eliyahu Ha-kohen (author of Shevet Musar). He also added a sort of source from the words of the Sages (Yalkut Shimoni, Mishlei 960): \"If you seek to take counsel from the Torah – take it; and so David says: 'I will converse about Your precepts.'\" Responsa Ḥayim She'al (part 2, 38:41) wrote, on the basis of Responsa of Rambam (172), that it is not proper for a person to open a book for a question of others. And Ḥesed La-alafim 179:4 added that without a special need one should not use opening a book even for oneself, but should walk wholeheartedly with the Lord."},{"n":10,"en":"The name \"Goral Ha-Gra\" was given in error. First, it was ruled in SA (YD) 179:1 that it is forbidden to use lotteries (goralot), and this method ought to be called \"opening a book.\" Second, it does not originate with the Gra, for during more than a hundred years after his passing there is no testimony in his name to this method (so too wrote the journal Or Yisrael 38, p. 254). Also in the study hall of our master Rav Kook, which was connected to the teachings of the Gra, we have found no hint of the use of devices of this sort. The only one of his devoted followers who sometimes used this method was the tzaddik Rav Aryeh Levin of blessed memory, and even that was in order to ease people's anguish, and not as a method of knowing the future (see the book \"Ish Tzaddik Haya,\" pp. 113-117).<br>In recent generations, however, beginning with the difficult periods of the First and Second World Wars, there were rabbis from Lithuania who used this method, among them the Ḥafetz Ḥayim, Rav Eliyahu Lopian and Rav Eliyahu Dessler. It is reported that they were accustomed to repent and pray in preparation for opening the book, so that the Lord would grant them a good answer. On the other hand, there were Lithuanian rabbis who opposed it, such as the Ḥazon Ish, and likewise his brother-in-law Rav Ya'akov Yisrael Kanievsky, of whom it was testified that he rebuked someone who performed a lottery, and said that there are people \"who made themselves miserable through the answers they received\" (Orḥot Rabbeinu, part 1, p. 218). Moreover, if it is indeed proper to use this method, then a solution to all questions has been found. A person who is uncertain whether to marry this woman or that one, or whether to open a business, would open a book and know. A community that is uncertain whether to choose this person or that one as head of the council or as rabbi – its representatives would open a book and decide. But it is clear that this position is untenable; only rarely, in rare situations of doubt that the intellect cannot resolve, did a few rabbis make use of opening a book. And even then, according to Rambam and those who agree with him, it is forbidden to act according to the opening of the book; at most one may take encouragement from it."}]},{"slug":"20-24-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-08/","title_he":"ח – גורלות, קלפי טארוט וקריאה בקפה","title_en":"Lotteries, Tarot Cards and Coffee Reading","body_en":"<p>It is forbidden to use a lottery (goral) in order to foretell the future or to advise a person how to act so that he will succeed, as it is stated (Devarim 18:13): \"You shall be wholehearted with the Lord your God\" (SA YD 179:1). That is, there are beliefs that by various supernatural means one can foretell the future and thereby be more successful in life, and the Torah commanded us not to follow them, but to rely on the Lord and on the natural talents He has given man. Included in the prohibition is also the use of tarot cards and coffee reading. Moreover, it seems that these things are also forbidden because of the prohibition of kosem or menaḥesh, whose essence is foretelling the future on the basis of performing acts or on the basis of events that happened to a person by chance (as explained above in halakhot 1-3). Let us explain a little about the common forbidden methods:</p><p>There are simple lotteries that predict the answer to a single question: whether, in order to succeed, the inquirer should do a certain thing or not. And there are complex lotteries based on astrology, according to which the experts in lotteries ascertain the inquirer's mazal according to the date and hour of his birth, and on that basis cast several lots in order to know things about his character, his future, and the good and bad paths before him. All these lotteries are forbidden by Torah law. True, there were Jewish masters of occult practices (ba'alei segulot) who wrote various lottery methods, and even attributed some of them to great early Sages of Israel, and some people act according to them to this day (see the note). But it is difficult to accept these traditions, since the poskim explained that this involves a Torah prohibition. In addition, it has become clear that many lotteries written in the books of Jewish masters of occult practices had previously been written in the books of Greek, Roman and Arab gentiles, and are rooted in traditions of sorcery.</p><p>Similar to lotteries, some use tarot cards, which are shuffled and laid face down on the table; the inquirer chooses one card or several cards at random, and according to the cards he chose, his future is foretold to him or he is advised how to act. All this is out of a belief that the random choice corresponds to the inquirer's fate, and expresses his inner world and what is in store for him in the future.</p><p>Coffee reading: in the conception of those who believe in it, when a person drinks a liquid, his spirit mingles with the liquid; and since in his subconscious his spirit knows the future that will come upon him, this knowledge passes into the liquid, and the shape of the stains that the liquid leaves on the cup contains hints of it. According to this belief, this is discernible in any drink that leaves stains, and coffee is used in places where it is common.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"Tosafot, Shabbat 156b, s.v. \"Kaldai\": \"From where do we know that one may not consult lotteries? As it is stated: 'You shall be wholehearted.'\" So too was it ruled in SA (YD) 179:1. It seems that one who foretells the future by means of lotteries, tarot cards or coffee reading, since he performs an action in order to foretell the future, also transgresses the negative prohibition of kosem or menaḥesh, as explained above on the basis of what was explained earlier in halakha 1.<br>However, among those close to the world of Kabbala and the esoteric, there are Torah scholars who use lotteries. In addition, there are books of lotteries that were in the hands of those who engaged in lotteries, which, according to them, were attributed to Rav Sa'adya Gaon, Rav Tzemaḥ Gaon, Ibn Ezra and others. Apparently, in their view the prohibition of making lotteries is when it is done through connecting to the forces of impurity in order to obtain the good things of this world; but when lotteries are made out of repentance, fear of Heaven and prayer to the Lord that He guide them to know how to act in the future, there is no prohibition of kosem or menaḥesh. Yet they too agree that when the lottery is done by people who are not God-fearing, and not out of repentance and prayer, then the forces of impurity become involved in it, and it is forbidden (similar to the view of those who permit inquiring of the spirit of the dead, because the prohibition is inquiring of their body, in which there is impurity; below 26:6). Likewise regarding the mitzva of \"You shall be wholehearted\": in their view the prohibition is to foretell the future with certainty, but since they believe in repentance and prayer, they do not determine the future conclusively, and therefore they do not transgress the mitzva of \"You shall be wholehearted.\" Similarly, those who read coffee and those who look at tarot cards could claim that when they do so out of prayer and arouse those who consult them to repentance, there is no prohibition in it. But according to the overwhelming majority of poskim, it is forbidden to use lotteries and the like in order to foretell the future; and even if the one making the prediction claims that it can be rectified through repentance and prayer, since he said his words on the basis of hidden means, the one who consults him can no longer conduct himself wholeheartedly with the Lord and weigh his actions rationally according to the Torah, as explained below (25:6-7).<br>For the purpose of division or determining a turn, since there is no attempt to foretell the future, it is permitted to use lotteries, such as a lottery for equal division between heirs or determining the turn for saying Kaddish, and like the lottery (payis) that was conducted in the Temple to determine the rota of the kohanim (Bava Batra 106b; SA ḤM 173:2; Magen Avraham OḤ 132:2; Ha-elef Lekha Shlomo OḤ 62). This is what is stated (Mishlei 18:18): \"The lot puts an end to quarrels and separates between the mighty.\" That is, the lot will put an end to the strife between the quarrelling parties, because by clarifying each one's share it will separate between the mighty ones who had previously grown strong in the quarrel. And let a person not think that it would have been better for him to overcome his opponent by force; rather, let him believe that the Lord watches over everything and accept what fell to him by lot, as it is stated (Mishlei 16:33): \"The lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the Lord.\"<br>There are lotteries that were performed by the word of the Lord, with prophetic guidance and through the Urim and Tumim, such as in the division of the Land, the capture of Akhan and the choice of Shaul as king; these are not to be compared to lotteries performed not by prophecy (Rav Ḥayim David Halevi in Mayim Ḥayim 1:19; Va-yeshev Ha-yam part 1, 13:9)."}]},{"slug":"20-24-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-09/","title_he":"ט – שיחת העופות","title_en":"The Language of Birds","body_en":"<p>The Sages said that King Shlomo knew how to understand the speech of birds, and this is what is stated (I Melakhim 5:10): \"And Shlomo's wisdom exceeded the wisdom of all the children of the East,\" who were familiar with the language of birds (Bamidbar Rabba 19:3). They likewise learned from the verse (Kohelet 10:20): \"For a bird of the heavens will carry the voice, and a winged creature will tell the matter\" – \"this is the raven and the wisdom of tayarin\" (the language of birds).</p><p>Similarly, there were Sages who were expert in the speech of trees, such as Rabban Yoḥanan ben Zakkai, of whom it was said that he knew the speech of palm trees (Sukka 28a). These branches of wisdom are based on the assumption that everything that is about to happen in the world is first known in the higher worlds, and there are various hints of this in the world, in the movement of the trees and in the chirping and flight of birds. Maharal explained that precisely animals, which are simpler than man, sometimes receive an impression from the higher realms and express it unwittingly in their own way; and birds, which are more spiritual, perceive more of the future (Netzaḥ Yisrael 26; Ḥiddushei Aggadot, Ḥullin 63a). In the days when mystical powers were more developed, there were those who learned these branches of wisdom (Sefer Ha-gilgulim 35; Shela, parashat Noaḥ, Torah Or).</p><p>It is told in the Talmud (Gittin 45a) of Rav Ilish, who was taken captive, and with him in captivity was a gentile who knew how to understand the language of birds. The gentile heard a raven and a dove saying: \"Ilish, flee! Ilish, flee!\" On this basis Rav Ilish decided to flee and was saved. But the gentile who understood the language of birds and fled with him was caught and killed. And although the Sages said (Sanhedrin 66a) that included in the prohibition of me'onen it is forbidden to divine by means of birds, the meaning is the superstition that if a person sees certain things in birds, this will be a sign for him that it is worthwhile to do business or set out on a journey, or the opposite. But one who truly knows the language of birds is permitted to take it into account (Ritva, Gittin 45a; Nimukei Yosef, Sanhedrin 15b, cited in Beit Yosef).</p><p>From Rambam and Ramban, however, it is implied that it is forbidden to perform an action on the basis of the speech of birds, and this prohibition is included in the prohibition of me'onen and menaḥesh (Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 11:4; Ramban, Vayikra 18:19; Maharsha and Ḥida on Sanhedrin 66a). According to their view, Rav Ilish relied on the speech of birds because he was in a situation of mortal danger (pikuaḥ nefesh) (Responsa Rashba 1:413; below 29, note 6).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-24-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-24-10/","title_he":"י – מופע קסמים לשם בידור","title_en":"A Magic Show for Entertainment","body_en":"<p>In recent generations it has become accepted to hold a \"magic\" show for entertainment, in which the magician makes objects disappear or turns them into other objects, or guesses numbers and facts about people watching the show. The question is whether this involves a prohibition.</p><p>According to the stringent view, it is forbidden to perform acts that look like sorcery by means of illusion (aḥizat einayim), even though in practice the \"magic\" is done by sleight of hand or other methods of deception. This prohibition is included in the prohibition of me'onen, for the Sages said (Sanhedrin 65b) that a me'onen is \"one who deceives the eyes.\" Likewise Abaye said (ibid. 67b), regarding the laws of sorcery: \"One who deceives the eyes – is exempt but it is forbidden\"; that is, it is forbidden to perform acts of sorcery by means of illusion, but since he did not actually perform sorcery, he is not punished for it with stoning like a sorcerer, or with lashes like a me'onen. According to the stringent view, since it is forbidden to put on a magic show, it is also forbidden to attend one, for whoever attends it helps cause the person performing the magic to stumble (Yeḥaveh Da'at 3:68; Shevet Ha-levi 5:129). However, when the magic show is put on by a gentile, according to most of the stringent authorities it is permitted to attend it, for the prohibition of sorcery applies to Israel and not to gentiles (Yabi'a Omer part 5, YD 14; Avnei Yashfeh part 3, YD 78:6).</p><p>Many poskim, however, were lenient regarding putting on a magic show and watching it. Some are lenient on condition that the magician informs his audience that all his acts are by illusion, for the prohibition applies only when people may think that this involves an act of sorcery beyond nature (Divrei Yatziv YD 1:57). And some require that the magician also demonstrate several tricks to the audience, in order to show them that in practice this is indeed sleight of hand and tricks of deception (Si'aḥ Naḥum 53; Ma'aseh Ish part 1, 10:27).</p><p>And some hold that there is no need for the magician to inform the audience of this; since everyone knows that he does not use forces of impurity to perform sorcery beyond the laws of nature, but rather his \"magic\" is done by sleight of hand and sophisticated tricks of deception, there is no prohibition in it. And everything that the Sages said is forbidden to do by illusion applies when the one performing the act claims that it is done by sorcery through supernatural powers (Igrot Moshe YD 4:13; Aseh Lekha Rav 2:44).</p><p>In practice, those who wish to be lenient may do so even without the magician needing to announce that his magic is illusion. True, in the past many in the audience believed that this was sorcery performed by supernatural powers, and therefore there was room to be stringent, and at least to require the magician to say that it is illusion. Today, however, it is known that this is not magic performed by supernatural forces of impurity.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Those who forbid hold that the Torah forbade performing wondrous acts, as Rambam wrote (Sefer Ha-mitzvot, negative commandment 32): \"Included in this warning too is the prohibition of the activity of the magicians (ḥartumim), and in the language of the Sages: 'A me'onen – this is one who deceives the eyes.' It is a great kind of trickery, joined with quickness of movement of the hand, until it appears to people that he is doing things that have no reality. As we see them constantly doing: he takes a rope and places it in the corner of his garment before the people, and brings it out as a snake; or he throws a ring into the air and afterwards brings it out of the mouth of one of the people standing before him. And whatever resembles these among the activities of the magicians that are well known among the masses – every such activity is forbidden. One who does this is called 'one who deceives the eyes,' and it is a kind of sorcery, and for this reason he receives lashes. And with this he also deceives people. And the damage that comes from this is very great, for depicting things that are utterly impossible as possible in the eyes of fools… is very bad, and will corrupt their minds and bring them to believe the impossible and that it can be possible.\" So too wrote Sefer Ha-ḥinukh 250. So too wrote SMaG (negative commandment 53) and SMaK (136). And although he has transgressed a Torah prohibition, he is not punished with stoning or lashes, because he performed no action, or because it is a lav that is given as a warning for a death penalty of the court, for which one is not punished (see above 19, note 1). So too wrote the Baḥ and the Shakh 179:17, following Rambam: \"For even illusion that is not by means of sorcery, but only by means of a mirror and tricks and quickness of movement of the hand – is forbidden.\" On this basis Ḥokhmat Adam 89:6 wrote that the jesters (badḥanim) who do things like these at weddings transgress a Torah prohibition. So too wrote in practice Yeḥaveh Da'at 3:68; Shevet Ha-levi 5:129; Avnei Yashfeh part 3, YD 78.<br>On the other hand, the view of those who are lenient is that all the kinds of illusion that were forbidden are specifically when they look like sorcery; but when it is clear that this is illusion without any help from forces of impurity or from higher spiritual powers, there is no prohibition. And the more widely this is known, the more authorities rule so in practice."}]}]});
